## Chaining Transformations into a Pipeline

A **pipeline** connects multiple data transformation stages. Each stage reads a collection, does one thing, and produces a new collection for the next stage.

### The Order Processing Pipeline

```xiom
type Order = {
  id: Int;
  amount: Int;
  cancelled: Bool;
}

fn filter_active_orders(orders: &Vec[Order]) -> Vec[Order] {
  let result: Vec[Order] = Vec[Order].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < orders.len() {
    let o = match orders.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    if !o.cancelled {
      result.push(o);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}

fn extract_amounts(orders: &Vec[Order]) -> Vec[Int] {
  let result: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < orders.len() {
    let o = match orders.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    result.push(o.amount);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}

fn filter_big_spenders(amounts: &Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] {
  let result: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < amounts.len() {
    let a = match amounts.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    if a > 50 {
      result.push(a);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}

fn double_amounts(amounts: &Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] {
  let result: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < amounts.len() {
    let a = match amounts.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    result.push(a * 2);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Assembling the Pipeline

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let orders: Vec[Order] = Vec[Order].new();
  orders.push(Order{ id: 1, amount: 30, cancelled: false });
  orders.push(Order{ id: 2, amount: 100, cancelled: false });
  orders.push(Order{ id: 3, amount: 80, cancelled: true });
  orders.push(Order{ id: 4, amount: 200, cancelled: false });

  // Pipeline: filter -> extract -> filter -> double
  let active = filter_active_orders(&orders);
  let amounts = extract_amounts(&active);
  let big = filter_big_spenders(&amounts);
  let premium = double_amounts(&big);

  io.println("Final premium amounts: " + string.int_to_str(premium.len()));
  return 0;
}
```

### Why Pipelines Win

Each stage is independently testable. You can debug one stage without understanding the others. You can insert a new stage (e.g., add tax) without rewriting anything. The stages are pure — they don't modify their inputs.

